      "passageId": "9f3a7c22",
      "passageHtml": "<strong>9f3a7c22</strong> <span style='color: red; margin-left: 8px; font-size:15px'>Hard</span><br/>\nThe neuropeptide oxytocin has been popularly called the \"love hormone\" because of its role in social bonding, trust, and pair bonding in mammals. However, recent research by psychologist Carsten De Dreu suggests that oxytocin may also promote in-group favoritism and out-group hostility. In a 2021 study, participants who received oxytocin were more likely to cooperate with members of their own group but less likely to trust strangers. De Dreu argues that oxytocin evolved not to promote general altruism but to enhance survival of one's own group, often at the expense of others."
    },
    {
      "passageId": "5d8b2e71",
      "passageHtml": "<strong>5d8b2e71</strong> <span style='color: red; margin-left: 8px; font-size:15px'>Hard</span><br/>\nThe philosopher Thomas Hobbes argued in <i>Leviathan</i> that humans in a state of nature are in a constant \"war of all against all,\" and that strong central authority is necessary to prevent societal collapse. [highlight-1]Jean-Jacques Rousseau, by contrast, contended that the earliest humans were solitary but peaceful, and that inequality and conflict emerged only with the invention of private property.[/highlight-1] This fundamental disagreement about human nature underlies much of modern political philosophy, from Locke to Rawls."
    },
    {
      "passageId": "2c9a4e63",
      "passageHtml": "<strong>2c9a4e63</strong> <span style='color: red; margin-left: 8px; font-size:15px'>Hard</span><br/>\nSome environmental economists argue that placing a monetary value on ecosystem services—such as pollination, water purification, and carbon sequestration—is essential for making conservation economically competitive with development. Critics contend that such valuation commodities nature and may lead to perverse outcomes, such as allowing pollution if the polluter pays. A third perspective, advanced by scholar Maria Santos, rejects both extremes: she proposes that valuation can be a useful rhetorical tool for advocacy without requiring that every service be priced. Santos's approach has been adopted in several international biodiversity agreements."
    },
    {
      "passageId": "7b1d9f58",
      "passageHtml": "<strong>7b1d9f58</strong> <span style='color: red; margin-left: 8px; font-size:15px'>Hard</span><br/>\nThe discovery of the tomb of Pharaoh Tutankhamun in 1922 by Howard Carter is often celebrated as a triumph of archaeology. But a 2019 re-examination of excavation records by Egyptologist Zahi Hawass reveals that Carter's team systematically underreported and destroyed many artifacts that were not made of gold or otherwise aesthetically valuable. Hawass argues that this selection bias has distorted our understanding of New Kingdom funerary practices, as scholars have relied on Carter's incomplete catalog. The lost items include textiles, food offerings, and everyday tools that would have provided crucial context."
    },
    {
      "passageId": "4e6c2a85",
      "passageHtml": "<strong>4e6c2a85</strong> <span style='color: red; margin-left: 8px; font-size:15px'>Hard</span><br/>\nThe following text is adapted from George Eliot's 1872 novel <i>Middlemarch</i>. The narrator is describing the character Dorothea Brooke.<br/>\n<br/>\nShe was open, ardent, and not in the least self-admiring; indeed, it was hard for her to believe that others thought well of her. [highlight-1]But she had a naive sense of her own importance that made her impatient of any hindrance to her ideal plans.[/highlight-1] This contradiction—humility about her person, arrogance about her purposes—made her both endearing and difficult to those around her."
    },
    {
      "passageId": "9d2f7b34",
      "passageHtml": "<strong>9d2f7b34</strong> <span style='color: red; margin-left: 8px; font-size:15px'>Hard</span><br/>\nIn his 1936 work <i>The Logic of Scientific Discovery</i>, Karl Popper proposed the criterion of falsifiability to distinguish science from non-science: a theory is scientific only if it can be proven false by some conceivable observation. For decades, this principle dominated philosophy of science. However, historian of science Thomas Kuhn argued in 1962 that science progresses not through falsification but through paradigm shifts, where anomalous data are initially ignored until a crisis forces a revolution. More recently, philosopher of science Paul Feyerabend claimed that no single method characterizes science; he famously stated \"anything goes.\""
    },
    {
      "passageId": "1a8c3e56",
      "passageHtml": "<strong>1a8c3e56</strong> <span style='color: red; margin-left: 8px; font-size:15px'>Hard</span><br/>\nResearchers studying the origin of life have long debated whether metabolism (chemical reactions) or replication (self-copying molecules) came first. A 2023 study by biochemist Serena Yang challenges both camps. Yang constructed a simple system of non-living organic molecules in a simulated hydrothermal vent. Over weeks, the system spontaneously developed autocatalytic cycles—reactions that produce catalysts for themselves—without any genetic material. Yang argues that this demonstrates a \"pre-biotic metabolism\" that could have preceded and later enabled the emergence of RNA and DNA."
    },
    {
      "passageId": "6e7b2c91",
      "passageHtml": "<strong>6e7b2c91</strong> <span style='color: red; margin-left: 8px; font-size:15px'>Hard</span><br/>\nLiterary theorist Mikhail Bakhtin introduced the concept of \"heteroglossia\" to describe the coexistence of multiple voices, languages, and social dialects within a single text. In a novel, heteroglossia can create tension between the author's voice and those of characters. [highlight-1]However, Bakhtin argued that no text is fully heteroglot; all writing is constrained by the generic conventions and ideological pressures of its time.[/highlight-1] This limitation, for Bakhtin, does not diminish the subversive potential of the novel but rather defines the arena in which that potential is realized."
    },
    {
      "passageId": "3d5a9f78",
      "passageHtml": "<strong>3d5a9f78</strong> <span style='color: red; margin-left: 8px; font-size:15px'>Hard</span><br/>\nIn the 1960s, psychologist Walter Mischel conducted the famous \"marshmallow test,\" in which young children who could resist eating a marshmallow for 15 minutes would receive a second. Longitudinal studies suggested that children who delayed gratification had better life outcomes. Recent replications have questioned these findings. A 2018 study with a larger, more diverse sample found that the predictive power of delay of gratification was greatly reduced after controlling for family background and early cognitive ability. Moreover, many children who ate the marshmallow immediately nevertheless succeeded later in life."
    },
    {
      "passageId": "8f2c4d17",
      "passageHtml": "<strong>8f2c4d17</strong> <span style='color: red; margin-left: 8px; font-size:15px'>Hard</span><br/>\nThe following text is adapted from Ralph Ellison's 1952 novel <i>Invisible Man</i>. The narrator is reflecting on his identity.<br/>\n<br/>\nI am invisible, understand, simply because people refuse to see me. [highlight-1]It is as though I am made of glass—they look through me, or rather, they see only the reflections of their own assumptions.[/highlight-1] When they approach me they see only my surroundings, themselves, or figments of their imagination—indeed, everything and anything except me."
    },
    {
      "passageId": "2b7e8a43",
      "passageHtml": "<strong>2b7e8a43</strong> <span style='color: red; margin-left: 8px; font-size:15px'>Hard</span><br/>\nIn the early 20th century, Austrian economist Joseph Schumpeter coined the term \"creative destruction\" to describe how capitalism incessantly revolutionizes the economic structure from within, destroying old industries and creating new ones. Many economists have embraced this as a source of dynamism. But economic historian Marina Mazzucato argues that Schumpeter's framework undervalues the role of the state. In her 2013 book <i>The Entrepreneurial State</i>, she shows that most of the foundational technologies behind the iPhone—including the internet, GPS, touchscreen, and Siri—were funded by government research. She contends that the state, not just private entrepreneurs, is often the primary risk-taker."
    },
    {
      "passageId": "7c4a9b65",
      "passageHtml": "<strong>7c4a9b65</strong> <span style='color: red; margin-left: 8px; font-size:15px'>Hard</span><br/>\nIn her study of early American cemeteries, historian Jill Lepore noticed that 17th-century New England gravestones often feature death's heads—skulls with wings—while 18th-century stones shift to cherubs and urns. [highlight-1]Lepore argues that this iconographic shift reflects the Great Awakening's emphasis on a loving, personal God rather than a stern judge.[/highlight-1] Later Puritan elites resisted this change, but the gravestone carvers, responding to popular demand, gradually adopted the cherub motif."
    },
    {
      "passageId": "5a1e8c24",
      "passageHtml": "<strong>5a1e8c24</strong> <span style='color: red; margin-left: 8px; font-size:15px'>Hard</span><br/>\nSome cognitive scientists have proposed that language is an innate, modular faculty—a \"universal grammar\" wired into the human brain. A competing view, known as usage-based linguistics, holds that language emerges from general cognitive processes such as memory, categorization, and social learning. A 2022 study by linguist Elena Lieven tested these predictions by training a neural network without any built-in grammar rules, only exposure to child-directed speech. The network successfully learned to produce grammatical sentences and even some irregular verb forms, supporting the usage-based account. Lieven concludes that no innate grammar module is necessary."
    },
    {
      "passageId": "9c6d2f31",
      "passageHtml": "<strong>9c6d2f31</strong> <span style='color: red; margin-left: 8px; font-size:15px'>Hard</span><br/>\nThe following text is adapted from Edith Wharton's 1920 novel <i>The Age of Innocence</i>. Newland Archer is reflecting on society's rules.<br/>\n<br/>\nHe understood that in old New York, a gentleman's duty was not to defy convention but to observe it with a kind of ironic grace. [highlight-1]To break the rules openly would be to admit they had power over you; to follow them perfectly, with a hint of detachment, was to master them.[/highlight-1] This was the secret that his mother had never been able to teach him, though she had lived it flawlessly."
    },
    {
      "passageId": "4e2b9a87",
      "passageHtml": "<strong>4e2b9a87</strong> <span style='color: red; margin-left: 8px; font-size:15px'>Hard</span><br/>\nResearch on the placebo effect has traditionally focused on conscious expectations—patients believe a treatment will work, so it does. But psychologist Karin Jensen's 2023 study introduced a twist. She gave participants a placebo cream while telling them it would either increase or decrease pain. Crucially, some participants were also given subliminal cues—flashes of happy or fearful faces too brief to consciously perceive. Those who received subliminal happy faces reported significantly less pain, regardless of what they consciously believed the cream would do. Jensen argues that non-conscious conditioning can produce placebo effects independent of conscious expectation."
    }
